From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 14:37:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] gx: textures never outlive or mismatch the memory they came
 from

A static texture missing from the content cache was hashed from game memory
and then uploaded from a second read of it. The FIFO is processed on its own
thread while the game runs on, and the game frees, reloads and compacts the
memory queued draws point at; when the bytes changed between the two reads,
the key of one picture held another for the rest of the session (a Toad
drawn with Flurrie's face). On a miss the bytes (and the TLUT's) are now
copied once, keyed again from the copy and uploaded from it.

EFB copies are found by their destination address. When the game freed or
moved a copy's buffer the record stayed, and whatever was allocated or moved
there next was drawn as the capture. AuroraForgetMemory(lo, hi) and
AuroraMoveMemory(from, to, size) (FIFO commands GX_AURORA_FORGET_MEMORY and
GX_AURORA_MOVE_MEMORY, in order with the draws) drop the copies made into
freed memory, and carry copies along with moved memory, dropping what was
recorded where it lands.

GXInvalidateTexAll, the game's word that texture memory changed under its
texture objects, was a no-op; it now drops the per-object texture cache, so
an object is made again from its bytes (through the content cache).
